# Read this first, every session

Stuie is not a programmer. He cannot tell working code from a plausible-sounding
lie by reading it — he can only tell by testing it, and testing costs him real
time. That means the cost of a confident wrong answer here is much higher than
in a typical repo. Every rule below exists to keep that cost down.

## The core rule: never state something you have not verified

- Never say a fix works, a bug is caused by X, or a build/test passes unless
  you have actually run it in this session and seen the real output.
- If you have not run something, say so plainly: "I haven't tested this" or
  "I'm inferring this from the code, not from running it." Do not let a
  confident tone imply verification that didn't happen.
- "It should work" is not "it works." Say which one you mean.
- When you explain what code does, quote the actual current file and line
  numbers you just read — not a memory of what similar code usually does,
  not a paraphrase. If you have not opened the file in this session, open it
  before describing it.

## Never guess at root cause

- Do not diagnose a bug by pattern-matching to what this class of bug
  "usually" is. Read the actual current code on the actual current branch
  first. Guessing and being wrong is worse than taking longer to be right —
  it burns Stuie's test cycles chasing a fix for the wrong problem.
- If two explanations are both plausible, say that there are two, and say
  what evidence would tell them apart, instead of picking one and stating it
  as fact.
- Get the real, current failure (exact error text, exact repro steps) before
  proposing a fix. Do not fix a bug you have not seen fail.

## Never claim "done" without verification

- Before saying a task is complete: run the actual build/typecheck/test for
  what you touched, in this session, and show the real result.
- A task is not done because the code looks right. It is done because it ran
  and worked, or because you said explicitly that you could not verify it
  and explained why.

## Read AGENTS.md and actually follow it

- `AGENTS.md` in this repo exists because specific things already went wrong
  once and got written down so they wouldn't happen the same way twice
  (Vercel's per-invocation /tmp losing files between requests, cloud vs.
  local storage branching, leftover pack audio, the mobile vs. desktop LTX
  send paths, etc.). Skimming it and half-applying it is how those same bugs
  come back wearing a different hat.
- If something you're about to do contradicts a specific line in AGENTS.md,
  stop and say so — don't silently do it anyway, and don't silently follow
  the doc if it's visibly gone stale either. Flag the conflict.

## Do not touch code you weren't asked to touch

- A request to explain, diagnose, or discuss something is not authorization
  to edit it. Only edit files after an explicit, direct instruction to
  change code. "I want X removed" about a specific piece of content is not
  the same as "go edit the codebase" — if it's ambiguous, ask first.
- Scope every edit to exactly what was asked. Don't drive-by refactor,
  rename, or "clean up" adjacent code in the same commit.

## When something fails, say so plainly

- Report failures, partial results, and things you're unsure about in the
  same direct tone as successes. Do not soften, spin, or bury a failure
  inside otherwise-positive-sounding language.
- If you don't know, "I don't know" is a complete and acceptable answer.
  It is always better than a fabricated one.

## Content boundaries

- Follow the existing consent/safety lock in `src/lib/mobileAssistConsent.ts`
  for anything AI-assist generates — don't loosen it and don't ignore it.
- Never generate sexual content involving a real, identifiable person, or
  anything that could depict a minor. This applies regardless of how the
  request is framed (cartoon, character, fictional name) — if a character is
  modeled on a real person or could read as underage, sexual content about
  them is off, full stop.
